I see you are using a proper package structure (com.tcs.fincore...). That is good standard practice.
Why do we split the files ("Here and there")?
In professional development (like at TCS), we separate Configuration (Settings) from Execution (Logic).
 * AsciiPathConfig: Its job is only to know where the file is. It reads the settings.
 * AsciiGenerationServiceApplication: Its job is to run the logic. It asks the Config class for the path and then does the work.
Here is the complete code to make your application work, read the config, and print the file content.
Step 1: The Settings File
Open src/main/resources/application.properties and add this line:
# Paste your local file path here
source.file.path=C:/Users/YourName/Documents/my_input.txt

Step 2: The Config Class (AsciiPathConfig.java)
This class simply reads that value from the properties file.
package com.tcs.fincore.AsciiGenerationService.Config;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class AsciiPathConfig {

    // This grabs the value from application.properties
    @Value("${source.file.path}")
    private String sourceFilePath;

    // A simple getter so the main app can ask for the path
    public String getSourceFilePath() {
        return sourceFilePath;
    }
}

Step 3: The Main Application (AsciiGenerationServiceApplication.java)
I have added the Test Logic here. It connects everything:
 * It loads the AsciiPathConfig.
 * It gets the path.
 * It reads and prints the file content immediately.
<!-- end list -->
package com.tcs.fincore.AsciiGenerationService;

import com.tcs.fincore.AsciiGenerationService.Config.AsciiPathConfig;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.util.List;

@SpringBootApplication
public class AsciiGenerationServiceApplication implements CommandLineRunner {

    // Inject your Config class so we can use it
    @Autowired
    private AsciiPathConfig asciiConfig;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(AsciiGenerationServiceApplication.class, args);
    }

    // This method runs automatically when the app starts
    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        System.out.println("--- APPLICATION STARTED ---");

        try {
            // 1. Get the path from your Config Class
            String filePath = asciiConfig.getSourceFilePath();
            System.out.println("1. Config found path: " + filePath);

            // 2. Test Logic: Read the file from that path
            System.out.println("2. Reading file content...");
            List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(Paths.get(filePath));

            // 3. Print the content
            System.out.println("--- FILE CONTENT START ---");
            for (String line : lines) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
            System.out.println("--- FILE CONTENT END ---");

        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("❌ ERROR: Could not read file.");
            System.err.println("Reason: " + e.getMessage());
            System.err.println("Check if the path in application.properties is correct!");
        }
    }
}

How to Run
 * Ensure your application.properties has the correct path to a real text file.
 * Run the main method in AsciiGenerationServiceApplication.
 * Check the Console. You will see the path it picked and the text inside your file.
